1.21(1) (1) procedures for training Persons and Staff on Persons' rights. Some examples of human right include but are not limited to: when a Person goes to bed, who/what/where a Person eats or drinks, who a Person talks to, who/when a people visit, free time, relationships, locked doors, access to money, access to community, Informed Choice in Competitive Integrated Employment, alcohol (age 21 or older), access to medication, and age appropriate media content;
1.21(2) (2) procedures for prevention of abuse and rights violations;
1.21(3) (3) processes for modifying rights when necessary;
1.21(4) (4) processes to review supports that have high risk for rights violations;
1.21(5) (5) responsibilities of the Contractor's human rights committee, which include the review of rights issues related to the services and supports the Contractor provides, and recommendations to the Person and the Person's team regarding the Person's human rights. The Contractor's human rights committee shall maintain minutes of its proceedings, and shall disclose those minutes to any state or federal auditor, reviewer, or DHHS representative within 24 hours of request;
1.21(6) (6) provisions for all Persons served by the Contractor to have the right to request a review by the Contractor's Human Rights Committee concerning supports or services to the Person; and
1.21(7) (7) provisions to ensure that the Contractor will support any court ordered human rights modification without violating any other of the Person's human rights unless authorized by the Contractor's Human Rights Committee.

1.22(a)(1) (1) discharge reason;
1.22(a)(2) (2) summary of services provided;
1.22(a)(3) (3) the name of and title of the Staff preparing the summary;
1.22(a)(4) (4) any obligations the Person may have for liabilities to the Contractor;
1.22(a)(5) (5) an inventory of any medications being sent with the Person; and

1.22(b)(1) (1) a list of current medication and a plan to continue medications without interruption;
1.22(b)(2) (2) any evident health and safety issues that led to the discharge or that were addressed while providing services;
1.22(b)(5) (5) any food or drug allergies;
1.22(b)(6) (6) Staff ratios, including preference regarding when the Person feels they need the most support;
1.22(b)(7) (7) preferred activities and support needs;
1.22(b)(8) (8) preferred living location, living environment, roommates (if any) and information regarding housing voucher programs, if applicable;
1.22(b)(9) (9) an inventory of the Person's personal belongings, including such items as their birth certificate, and social security card; and
1.22(b)(10) (10) all court orders and court obligations.

1.23(a)(1) (1) medical concerns, serious illnesses, allergies the Person suffers from, and chronic conditions and complaints;
1.23(a)(2) (2) swallow reflex issues;
1.23(a)(3) (3) authorization for any emergency medical treatment needed; and
1.23(a)(4) (4) any advanced directives.

1.23(b)(1) (1) medications are properly stored according to the Person's needs and capabilities, as determined by the Person's PCPT;
1.23(b)(2) (2) theft prevention and abuse of medication;
1.23(b)(3) (3) training and explanation to the Person regarding the prescribed medication indication, the correct dose, how to properly administer the medicine, and the schedule for taking the medication according to the prescription and healthcare professional;
1.23(b)(4) (4) supervision of the Person while the Person takes their medication; and

1.23(c)(1) (1) the Person's prescription medication must be packaged and dispensed to the Person by a licensed pharmacy using dose packaging when dose packaging is available. If dose packaging is not available, the Contractor may provide medication supports with medication that is dispensed in the original and lawful packaging of the medication with prior written approval from the DSPD Director or designee;
1.23(c)(2) (2) medication disposal;
1.23(c)(3) (3) process to ensure the transfer of prescription medication for services provided to the Person by a school or another service provider;
1.23(c)(4) (4) provisions to report or address the discovery of any prescribed medication errors.; and
1.23(c)(5) (5) enhanced process for monitoring the dispensing, tracking, and written documentation in Person's medical data sheet of Schedule II- IV medication under Title II of the Comprehensive Drug Abuse Prevention and Control Act of 1970, 21 U.S.C., 13, Subchapter I Part B §812,. The enhanced process for monitoring must include provisions for ensuring the medication count is accurate, and for theft and abuse prevention.

1.23(d)(1) (1) the name and purpose of each medication the Person is taking;
1.23(d)(3) (3) medication adversities, side effects, and indications of an effect or adverse reaction for each medication, including if there is a possibility that medication taken may contribute to swallowing difficulties or enhance the prospects of choking; and
1.23(d)(4) (4) documentation of compliance with medication administration requirements.

1.23(h)(1) (1) medical and/or dental examinations performed, including assessments or treatments;
1.23(h)(2) (2) current condition and diagnoses for which the Person is receiving care from a healthcare professional;
1.23(h)(3) (3) surgeries, accidents, injuries, and incidents requiring first aid and a referral to a health care professional or a healthcare facility;
1.23(h)(4) (4) immunizations; and
1.23(h)(5) (5) significant changes in health.

1.25(1) (1) Person's name;
1.25(2) (2) each service provided to the Person;
1.25(3) (3) date range the quarterly summary covers;
1.25(4) (4) general summary of services provided, the Person's status and response to services, and notable events and activities related to the services provided;
1.25(5) (5) information that indicates the progress toward each goal except for the following services: ELS, MTP, PBA, Professional Medication Monitoring and Respite; and
1.25(6) (6) name of the Staff writing the quarterly summary.

1.30(3)(A) (A) persons are not left unattended in the vehicle;
1.30(3)(B) (B) persons use seat belts and remain seated while the vehicle is in motion;
1.30(3)(C) (C) keys are removed from the vehicle when the driver is not in the driver's seat unless the driver is actively operating a lift on the vehicle that requires the keys to be in the ignition to operate the lift;
1.30(3)(D) (D) Persons in wheelchairs use seat belts or locking mechanisms to immobilize the wheelchair during travel;
1.30(3)(E) (E) vehicles used for transportation of Persons have working door locks, and doors are locked while the vehicle is moving;
1.30(3)(F) (F) Persons arrive safely at the arranged destination and at the scheduled time; and

1.32(b)(A) (A) all Persons and their guardian (when applicable);
1.32(b)(B) (B) all associated Support Coordinators; and
1.32(b)(C) (C) the DHHS/DSPD Director or designee through the submission of the "Group Service Review" form on the DHHS/DSPD website to dspd@utah.gov with the subject line "Group Service Review".
